e:T441,The role of visual context is supported by research on multimedia learning. <a href="https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/multimedia-learning/7A62F072A71289E1E262980CB026A3F9" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mayer's (2009) Cognitive Theory of Multimedia Learning</a> demonstrates that combining verbal information with relevant visual information improves comprehension and retention by distributing processing across two channels rather than overloading one. In L2 contexts, <a href="https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/studies-in-second-language-acquisition/article/abs/incidental-vocabulary-acquisition-through-viewing-l2-television-and-factors-that-affect-learning/0E45A630F37C48A5BDB6CC3F725ADDC9" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Peters and Webb (2018)</a> found that learners acquired vocabulary incidentally through watching a full-length TV documentary, with visual context helping learners infer the meaning of unknown words. This is why TV and video are recommended for earlier phases — they provide a scaffolding layer that audio-only content does not.f:T455,The distinction between scripted and unscripted speech reflects real differences in how language is produced and processed. Scripted dialogue tends to be more clearly articulated, uses more standard grammar, and contains fewer disfluencies, while natural conversation is faster, includes false starts and overlapping turns, and uses non-standard forms. <a href="https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/studies-in-second-language-acquisition/article/abs/modeling-perceptions-of-the-accentedness-and-comprehensibility-of-l2-speech-the-role-of-speaking-rate/E22C75E244694BF6D6E083149FBEFA51" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Munro and Derwing (2001)</a> demonstrated that speaking rate significantly affects how comprehensible L2 speech is perceived to be, and their broader body of work shows that both rate and clarity are major factors in whether learners can successfully process what they hear. This is why the roadmap recommends progressing from scripted to unscripted content as comprehension ability grows — it allows learners to build processing speed gradually rather than being overwhelmed.1:["$","$Ld",null,{"page":{"id":"library--comprehensibility-factors","kind":"library-article","route":"/roadmap/library/comprehensibility-factors","sourceUrl":"https://refold.la/roadmap/library/comprehensibility-factors","sourceHash":"024bd4c8b1594c51952a76e4e96cc3d71fb8e559fa2bee34f2619dbe56fb37da","collectedAt":"2026-09-01T16:52:40.332Z","bodyMode":"authorized-full-body","title":{"en":"Comprehensibility Factors","zh":"影响可理解性的因素"},"description":{"en":"","zh":""},"segments":[{"id":"segment-0001","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Not all content is equally hard to understand. Many factors affect how comprehensible something is, and you can manipulate these factors to find content at the right difficulty level for your current ability.","zh":"并非所有内容都同样难以理解。许多因素都会影响内容有多容易理解，而你可以调整这些因素，找到难度适合自己当前能力的内容。"}},{"id":"contextual-factors","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Contextual Factors","zh":"语境因素"}},{"id":"segment-0003","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Visual context:</strong> Can you see what's happening? TV shows and movies provide visual cues (facial expressions, gestures, settings) that help you understand even when you miss words. Podcasts and audiobooks provide none.","zh":"<strong>视觉语境：</strong>你能看到正在发生什么吗？电视剧和电影会提供视觉线索（面部表情、手势、场景），即使漏听了一些词，也能帮助你理解内容。播客和有声书则完全没有这些线索。"}},{"id":"segment-0004","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Narrative predictability:</strong> Is the story easy to follow? Slice-of-life dramas have simple, predictable plots. Mystery thrillers have complex, surprise-driven plots. Predictability helps comprehension.","zh":"<strong>叙事可预测性：</strong>故事容易理解和跟进吗？日常生活剧的情节简单且容易预测；悬疑惊悚片的情节则复杂，并由各种意外推动。可预测性有助于理解。"}},{"id":"segment-0005","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Domain familiarity:</strong> Are you familiar with the topic from your native language? Content in a domain you know well is much easier to understand than content in an unfamiliar domain.","zh":"<strong>领域熟悉度：</strong>你是否已通过母语熟悉这个主题？你熟悉的领域中的内容，比陌生领域中的内容容易理解得多。"}},{"id":"segment-0006","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Prior knowledge of the story:</strong> Have you seen this before (in another language, or read a summary)? Knowing what happens makes the language much easier to process.","zh":"<strong>对故事的已有了解：</strong>你以前是否看过这个故事（以另一种语言），或读过故事梗概？知道接下来会发生什么，会让语言处理变得容易得多。"}},{"id":"linguistic-factors","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Linguistic Factors","zh":"语言因素"}},{"id":"segment-0008","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Audience level:</strong> Content made for children uses simpler language than content for adults. Content for learners (comprehensible input) is designed to be easy. Novels for adolescents are easier than those for adults. And so on.","zh":"<strong>受众水平：</strong>面向儿童的内容比面向成人的内容使用更简单的语言。为学习者制作的内容（可理解输入）本身就以易于理解为目标。青少年小说比成人小说更容易理解，其他内容也是如此。"}},{"id":"segment-0009","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Speech speed and clarity:</strong> News anchors speak clearly and at moderate speed. Casual conversation between friends is fast, mumbled, and full of slang.","zh":"<strong>语速和清晰度：</strong>新闻主播吐字清楚，语速适中。朋友之间的随意交谈则语速快、含混不清，而且充满俚语。"}},{"id":"segment-0010","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Scripted vs. unscripted:</strong> Scripted dialogue (TV, movies) is cleaner and more predictable than unscripted speech (vlogs, podcasts, real conversation).","zh":"<strong>有脚本与无脚本：</strong>有脚本的对话（电视剧、电影）比无脚本的言语（视频博客、播客、真实对话）更清晰，也更容易预测。"}},{"id":"segment-0011","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Dubbed vs. native:</strong> Dubbed content often uses simpler, more standard language than native content which might contain cultural references or plays on words.","zh":"<strong>配音内容与原生内容：</strong>配音内容通常比原生内容使用更简单、更标准的语言；原生内容则可能包含文化典故或文字游戏。"}},{"id":"using-these-factors","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Using These Factors","zh":"运用这些因素"}},{"id":"segment-0013","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"When choosing immersion content, stack the factors in your favor at first: visual context + familiar domain + scripted + moderate speed = highly comprehensible. As you improve, gradually remove supports: unfamiliar content + no visual context + fast casual speech = much harder, but you'll be ready for it.","zh":"刚开始选择沉浸内容时，要尽量让这些因素都对你有利：视觉语境＋熟悉的领域＋有脚本＋适中语速＝高度可理解。随着水平提高，再逐步撤掉辅助：陌生内容＋没有视觉语境＋快速的随意言语＝难得多，但到那时你已经准备好了。"}},{"id":"segment-0014","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The key insight: you can make almost any content more comprehensible by manipulating these factors. If something is too hard, find a version with more support (add subtitles, watch a recap first, choose a simpler genre). If something is too easy, remove support (turn off subtitles, try unfamiliar content).","zh":"关键在于：通过调整这些因素，你几乎可以让任何内容变得更容易理解。如果某项内容太难，就找一个辅助更多的版本（添加字幕、先看内容回顾、选择更简单的类型）。如果某项内容太容易，就减少辅助（关闭字幕、尝试陌生内容）。"}},{"id":"research-and-reasoning","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Research and Reasoning","zh":"研究与依据"}},{"id":"segment-0016","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<a href=\"https://www.sdkrashen.com/content/books/principles_and_practice.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Krashen's (1982) Input Hypothesis</a> proposed that acquisition occurs when learners are exposed to language just beyond their current level — but what makes input land at the right level isn't just the grammar or vocabulary. It's the full set of contextual and linguistic supports surrounding it.","zh":"<a href=\"https://www.sdkrashen.com/content/books/principles_and_practice.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Krashen（1982）的输入假说</a>提出，当学习者接触到略高于其当前水平的语言时，习得便会发生——但决定输入是否处于恰当水平的，并不只是语法或词汇，而是围绕输入的整套语境和语言辅助。"}},{"id":"segment-0017","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"$e","zh":"多媒体学习研究支持视觉语境所发挥的作用。<a href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/multimedia-learning/7A62F072A71289E1E262980CB026A3F9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Mayer（2009）的多媒体学习认知理论</a>表明，将言语信息与相关视觉信息相结合，可以把处理任务分配到两个通道，而不是让单一通道超负荷，从而提升理解能力和记忆保持。在第二语言环境中，<a href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/studies-in-second-language-acquisition/article/abs/incidental-vocabulary-acquisition-through-viewing-l2-television-and-factors-that-affect-learning/0E45A630F37C48A5BDB6CC3F725ADDC9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Peters 和 Webb（2018）</a>发现，学习者在观看一部长篇电视纪录片时会附带习得词汇，而视觉语境能帮助他们推断生词的含义。因此，我们建议在较早阶段观看电视和视频——它们能提供纯音频内容所不具备的一层支架。"}},{"id":"segment-0018","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"$f","zh":"有脚本与无脚本言语之间的区别，反映了语言在产出和处理方式上的真实差异。有脚本的对话往往发音更清晰、语法更标准，口吃和停顿等不流畅现象也更少；自然对话则语速更快，包含说到一半重新开始、话轮重叠以及非标准形式。<a href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/studies-in-second-language-acquisition/article/abs/modeling-perceptions-of-the-accentedness-and-comprehensibility-of-l2-speech-the-role-of-speaking-rate/E22C75E244694BF6D6E083149FBEFA51\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Munro 和 Derwing（2001）</a>证明，语速会显著影响人们对第二语言言语可理解度的判断；他们更广泛的研究也表明，语速和清晰度都是决定学习者能否成功处理所听内容的重要因素。因此，路线图建议随着理解能力提高，逐步从有脚本内容过渡到无脚本内容——这样，学习者可以循序渐进地提升处理速度，而不至于不堪重负。"}}],"cards":[]}}]
